 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# OAuth2 Client: Scope not included in refresh token request\n\n## Description\nWhen using the OAuth2 Client to prepare a refresh token request, the scope parameter is not being included in the request body unless it's explicitly provided in the method call. This breaks backward compatibility with previous behavior where the client's default scope would be used when no scope is provided.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.oauth2 import Client\n\n# Create a client with a default scope\nclient = Client('client_id', scope='default_scope')\n\n# Prepare a refresh token request without explicitly providing a scope\nurl = 'https://example.com/token'\nrefresh_token = 'my_refresh_token'\nurl, headers, body = client.prepare_refresh_token_request(url, refresh_token)\n\nprint(body)  # The scope parameter is missing from the body\n```\n\n## Expected Results\nThe request body should include the client's default scope when no scope is explicitly provided:\n```\ngrant_type=refresh_token&scope=default_scope&refresh_token=my_refresh_token\n```\n\n## Actual Results\nThe request body does not include the scope parameter at all:\n```\ngrant_type=refresh_token&refresh_token=my_refresh_token\n```\n\nThis is a breaking change from previous behavior where the client's default scope would be used when no scope was provided to the method.\n\n## Environment\n- oauthlib version: 3.2.0\n- Python version: 3.8.10\n\n## Additional Context\nThis change affects applications that rely on the client's default scope being included in refresh token requests. Applications now need to explicitly provide the scope parameter every time, even if it's the same as the client's default scope.\n</issue>\n",
